# Cross-repo siblings cloned by setup-dev-env at workspace-lock-pinned
# revisions (see codetracer-specs/Testing/Cross-Repo-CI-Integration.md and
# metacraft-dev-guidelines/policies/ci-shared-dev-env.md section 3.2).
#
# ONE REPO NAME PER LINE, NO REVISIONS.  That is the whole point of the file:
# `clone-siblings` resolves each name against the per-commit workspace lock
# published for the commit under test, so re-running an old commit's job clones
# the siblings THAT commit was built against.  A `name=ref` entry would pin a
# branch TIP instead -- a different revision on every re-run -- which is what
# `clone-siblings` emits a `::warning:: ... not reproducible` for.
#
# WHAT THIS LIST IS.  The cross-repo siblings of the launcher <-> recorder
# end-to-end record gate (codetracer-specs/Testing/
# Launcher-Recorder-Compatibility-Tests.md, milestone LRC-6): hop 1, hop 3 and
# the trace-validation oracle.  `codetracer` itself is deliberately absent -- a
# repo is never its own sibling, and `clone-siblings` would `rm -rf` the
# primary checkout if it were.
#
# WHAT READS IT.  `clone-siblings` reads `$GITHUB_WORKSPACE/.github/sibling-repos`
# only when its `siblings:` input is EMPTY.  Every setup-dev-env call site in
# this repo passes an explicit `siblings:` subset today, so this file changes
# no existing job -- `ci/test/sibling-provisioning-test.sh` asserts that, so
# the day a job stops passing one is the day this list becomes its clone set,
# deliberately and visibly.
#
# `.github/workflows/launcher-recorder-e2e.yml` passes a subset of this list:
# the gate needs ONE recorder per matrix row, not all three, and on the
# launcher and recorder edges it must add `codetracer` (which is a sibling
# there) and drop the triggering repo (which cannot be one).  The same test
# file asserts that every name that workflow can emit is declared here, so the
# two cannot drift.
codetracer-launcher
codetracer-trace-format-nim
codetracer-python-recorder
codetracer-ruby-recorder
codetracer-js-recorder
